def LAD(*a): return list(a)
def RUB(a,b,c,d): return [(a,'Missing or unclear','Meets the stated check',b),(c,'Guessing','Evidence kept',d),('Records','Gaps','Complete','Complete and reviewed by a peer'),('Safety and ethics','Overlooked','Followed','Followed and taught to others')]
COURSES = [
dict(slug='automobile', title='Automobile and Electric Vehicle Servicing', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Learn how a modern vehicle works, how to inspect and service it safely, find faults with measurements, and run a small workshop that keeps records and keeps customers.',
 outs=[('Define','the main systems of a petrol, diesel and electric vehicle.'),('Apply','a safe inspection and service routine with a written job card.'),('Analyse','faults using symptoms, readings and a fault tree.'),('Evaluate','repair-or-replace choices on cost, safety and life left.'),('Create','a workshop plan with bays, tools, records and customer follow-up.')],
 task='Inspect, diagnose and service a light vehicle, and record the work so another technician can repeat it.',
 cond='About 10 weeks part-time. A workshop bay or training vehicle, basic hand tools, a torque wrench, a scan tool, a multimeter, a mentor on site.',
 standard='Every job follows the written safety steps, readings are logged with units and limits, the fault is proved by a measurement before parts are changed, and the customer receives a clear job card.',
 who='School leavers, ITI and diploma students, mechanics moving to electric vehicles, small workshop owners.', req='None; basic reading and arithmetic.',
 mods=[
 M('Shop safety and vehicle systems','Define',4,['Name the main systems: engine or motor, transmission, brakes, steering, suspension, cooling, electrical and body.','Wear gloves, safety shoes and eye protection; keep a dry-powder or CO2 extinguisher within ten steps of every bay.','Lift a vehicle only at the marked points, chock the wheels, and lower it onto rated stands before going under.','Orange cables mean high voltage; only trained people with the written isolation steps may touch them.'],'A 1,100 kg hatchback on a two-post lift: pads set under the marked points, wheels chocked, lift raised 30 cm, shaken gently to test balance, then raised to 1.8 m and locked.','Draw a labelled map of the eight systems and mark the lift points on a training vehicle.','All eight systems are named correctly and the lift points match the owner manual.','What must be done before anyone goes under a lifted vehicle?','Check the lift points, chock the wheels and lock the lift or fit rated stands.'),
 M('Engine and motor basics','Explain',5,['Petrol engines run four strokes (intake, compression, power, exhaust) and ignite with a spark; diesels ignite by compressing air to about 30 to 40 times atmospheric pressure.','An electric motor turns when the controller sends current to the stator; speed and torque follow the controller setting.','A hybrid shares the drive between engine and motor, and software picks the mix.','Warning lights and odd sounds are first clues, not the answer; read the code and test before acting.'],'A car shakes at idle and shows a misfire code for cylinder 2. The technician swaps the cylinder 2 and 3 ignition coils; the code moves to cylinder 3, so the coil is faulty, not the injector.','Explain in 150 words how petrol, diesel and electric drive differ, using one diagram.','The explanation names the energy source, how power is made, and one service difference for each type.','Why swap two coils when a misfire code appears?','If the code moves with the coil, the coil is the fault; this proves it before buying a part.'),
 M('Inspection and routine service','Apply',5,['Follow the maker service schedule: oil and filter, air filter, coolant, brake fluid, tyre pressure and tread.','Tighten wheel nuts in a star pattern with a torque wrench set to the written value, for example 100 N m, then paint-mark each nut.','Write each check as done, adjusted, replaced or advised, with the reading and unit beside it.','Collect used oil, filters and batteries in labelled containers and hand them to an authorised collector.'],'A routine job card: engine oil 3.5 L replaced, filter replaced, tyre pressures set to 2.2 bar front and 2.4 bar rear, brake pad 4 mm advised for the next visit at 10,000 km.','Complete a job card for a full service on a training vehicle.','Every line shows done, adjusted, replaced or advised, and each reading has a unit.','Why paint-mark nuts after torquing?','A mark shows at a glance that the nut was tightened and has not moved.'),
 M('Brakes, steering and suspension','Apply',5,['Measure pad lining and disc thickness with a vernier or micrometer and compare with the minimum stamped on the disc or in the manual.','Bleed brakes in the maker order, longest line first, keep the reservoir topped up, and press the pedal firm before the road test.','Check play in ball joints, tie-rod ends and bushes with the wheel lifted; look for oil on dampers.','Alignment is measured on a machine; never guess it by eye.'],'Pad lining measures 2.5 mm against a 3 mm minimum, and the disc is 21.2 mm against a 20.0 mm minimum. Replace both pads on the axle, keep the discs, and test at low speed in an empty yard before the road.','Carry out a brake inspection and write pass or fail with numbers.','Readings are in millimetres with the limit beside them and the test drive is recorded.','Why replace pads on both wheels of an axle together?','Uneven pads pull the car to one side and brake unevenly.'),
 M('Electrical and diagnostics','Analyse',5,['Use a multimeter for voltage, resistance and continuity; a healthy 12 V battery rests near 12.6 V and charges at 13.8 to 14.4 V with the engine running.','Read fault codes and live data; a code points to a circuit, not always to a part.','Fault tree: symptom, likely causes, cheapest test first, confirm before replacing.','Disconnect the battery negative first and reconnect it last; keep tools away from the positive terminal.'],'No-start with a clicking relay: battery rests at 11.6 V, falls to 8.9 V under a load test, and the charging voltage is 14.1 V. The battery is faulty, not the starter or alternator.','Diagnose a seeded fault using a fault tree and write the proof.','The fault is confirmed with a measurement and its unit before any part is replaced.','Why test the cheapest cause first?','It saves time and money, and often solves the fault without buying parts.'),
 M('Electric vehicle systems','Analyse',5,['Learn the parts: battery pack, cells, battery management system, onboard charger, inverter, motor and thermal loop.','Charging types: slow AC home charging at about 3 kW and fast DC public charging at 30 kW or more; know the connectors used in India.','Range depends on speed, load, temperature, tyre pressure and driving style.','Only trained and certified people open high-voltage parts; others read data, check visible cables and keep the car switched off.'],'A customer says range fell from 250 km to 200 km. Data shows tyre pressure 1.6 bar against 2.3 bar and air conditioning always on. Pressure is corrected and range returns to about 225 km.','Explain to a customer, in plain words, why range changes, with three causes.','Three causes are named, each with a simple action the customer can take.','Who may open a high-voltage battery pack?','Only trained and certified people following the written isolation procedure.'),
 M('Workshop operations and records','Evaluate',5,['Draw the bay plan, tool list, tool calibration dates and spare stock list.','Quote before work with two options and prices, get written approval for extra work, rank safety items (brakes, tyres, steering, lights) first, and invoice with GST on time.','Track comebacks, meaning the same fault returning within 30 days, as the main quality measure.','Call the customer after three days and thirty days.'],'A workshop completes 120 jobs in a month with 4 comebacks, a rate of 3.3%. Two came from one rushed technician, so the owner adds a second check on brake work and the next month shows 1 in 130.','Build a one-page workshop plan with bay layout, records and two quality measures.','The plan shows the layout, record forms and two measures with a monthly target.','How is a comeback rate worked out?','Comebacks divided by total jobs, multiplied by 100.'),
 M('Capstone: inspect, diagnose, report','Create',4,['Choose a training vehicle and a scenario.','Inspect, diagnose, repair or advise, and write the report.','Present the report to a mentor and a mock customer.','Reflect on what you would do differently.'],'A final report with three findings, two repairs done, one advised for later, total time 3.5 hours, and the customer questions answered.','Run the full job and present it in five minutes.','Job card, readings, proof of fault and customer summary are all present and correct.','What does a good final report contain?','Findings, actions, readings, advice and a clear customer summary.')],
 extra=[('Name the first step before any lift.','Check the lift points and chock the wheels.'),('Why log units next to readings?','So anyone can repeat or check the reading.')],
 rubric=RUB('Diagnosis','Fault proved by measurement and shared as a method','Customer','Clear summary and follow-up booked'),
 ladder=LAD('Know the parts and the safety rules.','Describe how engine, brakes and electric drive work.','Do routine checks under supervision.','Service and diagnose common faults alone.','Handle electric and hybrid diagnosis and complex faults.','Lead a workshop, train others and set quality measures.','Design workshop systems, standards and training for a region.'),
 jobs=['Vehicle service technician','Electric vehicle technician','Workshop supervisor','Service adviser','Small workshop owner']),

dict(slug='electronics', title='Electronics Assembly and Testing', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Build and test electronic circuits and boards, read schematics, solder safely, and report faults with measured proof.',
 outs=[('Define','voltage, current, resistance and power with units.'),('Apply','safe soldering and assembly to a written standard.'),('Analyse','a circuit with a multimeter and an oscilloscope.'),('Evaluate','a board for fit to its specification.'),('Create','a small tested product and its build record.')],
 task='Assemble a small powered board from a schematic, test it against its specification, and keep a build and test record.',
 cond='About 10 weeks part-time. A soldering station, multimeter, bench power supply, oscilloscope or logic probe, a kit of parts, a mentor.',
 standard='Joints meet the written solder standard, every test reading has a unit and a limit, faults are proved by measurement, and the build record lets another person repeat the work.',
 who='School leavers, ITI and diploma students, repair technicians, makers and small manufacturing staff.', req='None; basic arithmetic.',
 mods=[
 M('Bench safety and basic quantities','Define',4,['Define voltage in volts, current in amperes, resistance in ohms and power in watts, and give the symbol for each.','Wear eye protection when soldering and cutting leads, and work with ventilation or a fume extractor.','Set the iron in its stand, never on the bench, and switch it off when you leave.','Use an earthed mat and wrist strap when handling chips that static can damage; never work on mains-powered circuits with the supply connected.'],'A 5 V supply drives a 250 ohm resistor. Current is 5 / 250 = 0.02 A, or 20 mA, and power is 5 x 0.02 = 0.1 W, so a quarter-watt resistor is safe.','Work out voltage, current and power for three resistor circuits.','All three answers carry correct units and match a meter reading within 5%.','What is the current through 100 ohms at 5 V?','0.05 A, or 50 mA.'),
 M('Read schematics and components','Explain',5,['Learn the symbols for resistor, capacitor, diode, LED, transistor and chip.','Read resistor colour bands and capacitor markings, and check with a meter.','Follow a signal from input to output on a schematic with a finger or a highlighter.','Note polarity for diodes, LEDs and electrolytic capacitors before fitting.'],'A resistor with brown, black, orange, gold bands reads 10 x 1,000 = 10 kilo-ohm, plus or minus 5%, so acceptable readings are 9.5 to 10.5 kilo-ohm.','Identify twelve parts on a kit board and list value, polarity and position.','All twelve are identified and values agree with a meter within the stated tolerance.','What does the gold band mean?','A tolerance of plus or minus 5%.'),
 M('Solder and assemble','Apply',6,['Heat the pad and lead together for about 2 seconds, then feed solder; a good joint is shiny and shaped like a small cone.','Fit parts in order of height: resistors, diodes, chip sockets, capacitors, connectors.','Trim leads flush after soldering and clean flux residue with isopropyl alcohol.','Use lead-free solder where the product must meet RoHS and keep the work area clean.'],'A 40-joint board is built in 55 minutes; an inspection with a magnifier finds 2 cold joints and 1 bridge, which are reflowed; first-pass yield is 37 of 40, or 92.5%.','Assemble a board and inspect each joint under a magnifier.','At least 95% of joints pass the written standard on first inspection.','What does a dull, grainy joint show?','A cold joint, which can fail; reheat and add fresh solder.'),
 M('Measure with a multimeter','Apply',5,['Set the meter to the correct mode and range before touching the circuit.','Measure voltage in parallel and current in series; resistance only with power off.','Use continuity beep to check tracks and joints.','Write each reading with its unit and the expected range.'],'A 9 V battery feeds a 5 V regulator. Input reads 8.7 V and output 4.98 V, which is within 5% of 5 V, so the regulator is healthy.','Take ten readings on a powered board and compare each with its expected value.','Each reading has a unit, an expected range and a pass or fail.','Why is current measured in series?','The meter must carry the circuit current to measure it.'),
 M('Test with an oscilloscope','Analyse',5,['Set volts per division and time per division so the wave fills the screen.','Read amplitude, period and frequency from the display; frequency is one divided by period.','Use a x10 probe and compensate it before measuring.','Compare an input and an output wave to see gain, delay or distortion.'],'A square wave spans 5 divisions at 2 ms per division, so period is 10 ms and frequency is 1 / 0.01 = 100 Hz; the height of 2.5 divisions at 2 V per division gives 5 V peak to peak.','Capture and measure a waveform on a timing circuit.','Period, frequency and amplitude are calculated and match the design within 10%.','How do you find frequency from the period?','Divide one by the period.'),
 M('Find and prove faults','Analyse',5,['Start with a visual check for burnt parts, cracks and lifted pads.','Measure the supply first, then follow the signal stage by stage.','Change one thing at a time and record the result.','Prove the fault with a reading before replacing the part.'],'A board has no output. The 5 V rail reads 0 V; the input fuse shows no continuity (open); replacing the 1 A fuse restores 5.02 V and the output works.','Diagnose a board with a seeded fault and write the proof.','The fault is located by measurement and the repair is confirmed by a second reading.','Why change only one thing at a time?','Otherwise you cannot tell which change fixed the fault.'),
 M('Check against specification','Evaluate',5,['Turn the specification into a test sheet with limits for each requirement.','Test under normal and worst-case supply, for example 4.5 V and 5.5 V.','Record pass, fail or marginal, and flag marginal results for review.','Decide to accept, rework or reject, and give the reason.'],'A 5 V supply board must give 4.75 to 5.25 V at 0.5 A load. Tests give 4.98 V at 0.1 A and 4.80 V at 0.5 A, so it passes but the low end is marginal.','Write a test sheet and test one board against it.','Every requirement has a limit, a reading and a decision.','What is a marginal result?','A reading that passes but sits close to the limit.'),
 M('Capstone: build, test, document','Create',4,['Choose a small product such as a regulated power supply or an LED timer.','Build it from the schematic and test it fully.','Write the build record with parts, photos, readings and faults.','Present the product and answer questions.'],'A power supply is built in 3 hours with 48 joints, 2 reworked, 12 tests all passed, and a one-page record signed off by the mentor.','Present the build and test record in five minutes.','The record is complete enough for another person to repeat the build.','What belongs in a build record?','Parts, steps, readings, faults found and the final decision.')],
 extra=[('Name the first check before powering a new board.','Inspect for bridges and wrong polarity, then check resistance across the supply.'),('Why use a fume extractor?','Flux smoke irritates the lungs and eyes.')],
 rubric=RUB('Assembly','Joints meet the standard and the method is shared','Testing','Every reading has a unit and a limit'),
 ladder=LAD('Know the safety rules and basic quantities.','Read schematics and identify components.','Solder and assemble simple boards under supervision.','Test and repair boards alone.','Handle complex faults and specification tests.','Lead a production line and set test methods.','Design test systems and standards for a manufacturer.'),
 jobs=['Electronics assembly operator','Test technician','Repair technician','Quality inspector','Prototype maker']),

dict(slug='information-technologies', title='Information Technology Support and Practice', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Support computers, networks and basic software for a small organisation, keep data safe and solve user problems with records.',
 outs=[('Define','the parts of a computer, a network and an operating system.'),('Apply','set-up, update and backup tasks with a checklist.'),('Analyse','a user problem from symptoms to cause.'),('Evaluate','the risk of a setting or a habit.'),('Create','a support plan with a help log and a backup test.')],
 task='Set up, secure and support a small office of five users, keep a help log, and prove a backup can be restored.',
 cond='About 10 weeks part-time. Two or three practice computers, a router and switch, a spare drive, free software, a mentor.',
 standard='Each task follows a written checklist, each problem is logged with symptom, cause and fix, backups are restored in a test, and passwords and personal data are protected.',
 who='School leavers, diploma and degree students, office staff taking on support duties, small business owners.', req='None; basic computer use.',
 mods=[
 M('Computer parts and operating systems','Define',4,['Name the processor, memory, storage, power supply, motherboard and display, and what each does.','Explain the operating system as the layer that shares hardware between programs.','Check specifications in system settings: memory size, storage free, version.','Switch off and unplug before opening a case, and touch bare metal to discharge static.'],'A user complains of slowness. Settings show 4 GB memory with 3.8 GB used and 12% disk free; adding 4 GB memory and clearing 40 GB of files fixes the delay.','List the parts and specifications of a practice computer.','All parts are named correctly and the specifications match system settings.','What does the operating system do?','It shares hardware between programs and the user.'),
 M('Set up and update a computer','Apply',5,['Follow a written checklist: install the operating system, drivers, updates, office tools, and antivirus where needed.','Create a standard user account for daily work and keep the administrator account separate.','Turn on automatic updates and check them monthly.','Label the machine, record its serial number and note the date in an asset list.'],'A new office computer takes 90 minutes: 40 for the operating system, 30 for updates, 10 for software, 10 for the checklist and asset record.','Set up a practice computer from a checklist and sign each step.','Every step is ticked and the asset list entry is complete.','Why use a standard account for daily work?','It limits the damage a mistake or malware can do.'),
 M('Networks and internet','Explain',5,['A router joins the office network to the internet; a switch joins devices inside it.','Each device has an IP address; a typical office uses 192.168.1.x with the router at 192.168.1.1.','Use wired links for desktops and Wi-Fi with WPA2 or WPA3 for phones and laptops.','Test with ping and a speed test and note the results.'],'A five-user office has a 100 Mbps line. A speed test shows 12 Mbps at 5 pm; the cause is one machine downloading updates, so updates are scheduled for 8 pm.','Draw the network of a small office and test two connections.','The drawing shows router, switch and devices, and test results have units.','What does a switch do?','It connects devices inside the same network.'),
 M('Backup and recovery','Apply',5,['Follow the 3-2-1 rule: three copies, on two kinds of media, one stored elsewhere.','Schedule backups so that no more than one day of work is lost.','Restore a sample file every month to prove the backup works.','Encrypt backups that hold personal data and keep the key safe.'],'A 120 GB office folder grows by 2 GB a day. A weekly full backup plus daily changes needs about 120 + 12 GB = 132 GB per week, so a 500 GB drive holds nearly four weeks.','Set up a backup and restore three files from it.','All three files restore intact and the restore time is recorded.','What proves a backup works?','Restoring a file from it.'),
 M('Security and passwords','Evaluate',5,['Use long, unique passwords kept in a password manager, and turn on two-step sign-in.','Spot phishing: odd sender, urgent tone, mismatched link, request for a password or OTP.','Never share OTPs, and report suspicious mail to the support lead.','Follow the data protection rules for personal data and keep only what you need.'],'A user receives a mail asking to update bank details through a link. The link address does not match the sender, so it is reported and deleted, and no password is shared.','Review five emails and mark which are phishing, with reasons.','At least four of five are classified correctly with a reason for each.','Should anyone share an OTP with support staff?','No; an OTP is private to the user.'),
 M('Troubleshoot user problems','Analyse',6,['Ask what changed, when it started and what the user sees.','Reproduce the problem, then test the simplest cause first.','Fix one thing at a time and ask the user to confirm.','Record symptom, cause and fix in the help log.'],'A user cannot print. The printer shows online, but the queue holds 14 stuck jobs; clearing the queue and restarting the print service fixes it in 6 minutes.','Solve three seeded problems and log each.','Each log entry has symptom, cause, fix and time taken.','Why ask what changed?','Most faults follow a recent change.'),
 M('Help desk habits and records','Evaluate',5,['Answer politely, repeat the problem back and set a time to respond.','Rank requests by effect: no-one can work first, one user next, advice last.','Track the help log: number of tickets, average time to fix, repeat problems.','Write a short how-to guide for any problem seen three times.'],'A help log shows 30 tickets in a month; 9 are password resets. A self-service reset guide cuts them to 3 the next month.','Review a month of help log data and propose two changes.','Proposals link to specific numbers from the log.','What should be fixed first?','Problems that stop everyone from working.'),
 M('Capstone: support plan and test','Create',4,['Choose an office of five users.','Write a support plan with set-up checklist, backup schedule and security rules.','Run a restore test and a phishing check.','Present the plan, the help log and the results.'],'A plan covers 5 computers, a nightly backup, a monthly restore test, a help log and a one-page rule sheet; the restore test recovers 100% of 10 sample files.','Present the plan and the test results in five minutes.','The plan is complete, the restore test passes and the log is used.','What proves the support plan works?','A passed restore test and a help log that shows problems solved.')],
 extra=[('Name the first step before opening a computer case.','Switch off, unplug and discharge static.'),('Why keep a help log?','It shows repeat problems and helps decide what to fix.')],
 rubric=RUB('Support','Fixes problems fast and writes guides others use','Security','Rules set and checked regularly'),
 ladder=LAD('Know the parts and safe habits.','Describe how networks and operating systems work.','Set up and update computers under supervision.','Support a small office alone.','Handle networks, backups and security for a business.','Lead an IT team and set policy.','Design support systems and standards across organisations.'),
 jobs=['IT support technician','Help desk agent','Network assistant','Office IT administrator','Computer hardware technician']),
]
